  "text": "None of the three solutions gives a final answer I can confirm, so I'm choosing NONE.\n\n**Lower bound from the path.** On a path, every interior vertex has degree 2, so the update is the midpoint for every p > 1. The dynamics is then p-independent and matches the p = 2 linear Gauss–Seidel iteration. Its slowest mode has spectral gap about n⁻², so the expected time is at least about n³ log(1/ε). That gives β_p ≥ 3 for all p, which rules out Solution 3's p + 1, since p + 1 < 3 for p < 2.\n\n**Solution 2 (β_p = 3).** The path bound is correct, but the upper bound is asserted rather than shown. The claim that higher-degree graphs are never worse is unproven. The question also asks for β_p \"as an explicit function of p\" for p < 3, which suggests the answer is not constant.\n\n**Solution 1 (max(3, p/(p−1))).** The complete-graph heuristic is not convincing. A rough linearization around the cluster center suggests the complete graph gives about n² ε^{p−2}, which does not produce the claimed n^{p/(p−1)} scaling. The ODE step is asserted without derivation. The formula also stays constant at 3 on (3/2, 3), which sits awkwardly with the question's phrasing about p < 3.\n\nI could not derive the correct exponent, so I can't say which exact function is right. None of the three proposed answers is supported by the analysis.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
